1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,023
What happens inside the bank when it takes money from a card?

2
00:00:03,023 --> 00:00:07,404
The simplest way is to send one debit request straight to the processing center.

3
00:00:07,404 --> 00:00:13,750
But if two requests run at the same time, or the page is submitted twice, the card is charged twice.

4
00:00:13,750 --> 00:00:21,547
So before any money moves, the payment switches from open to authorizing in one atomic update, and only one request can win it.

5
00:00:21,547 --> 00:00:28,059
Every call to the processing center is written to an operation journal, with its result and reference numbers.

6
00:00:28,059 --> 00:00:33,821
The bank sends the debit through its terminal, identified by a merchant ID and a terminal ID.

7
00:00:33,821 --> 00:00:40,000
The debit runs as authorization and capture: first the money is held, then it is captured.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,714
When the processing center approves, the payment becomes captured, with its retrieval reference number.

